              <span id="alg5.l9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg5.l9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
<div id="alg5.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg5.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg5.l10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg5.l10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg5.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg5.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
              
              
            Obtain <math id="alg5.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> via (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In 3.1 SDP-based Lipschitz Estimates with General Slope Bounds and Arbitrary Input-Output Indices ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ ECLipsE-Gen-Local: Efficient Compositional Local Lipschitz Estimates for Deep Neural Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) using <math id="alg5.l11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\alpha}^{i,\mathrm{adj}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝜶</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>adj</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\alpha}^{i,\mathrm{adj}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="alg5.l11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\beta}^{i,\mathrm{adj}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝜷</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>adj</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\beta}^{i,\mathrm{adj}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg5.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg5.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
              
              
            Set <math id="alg5.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{i}\leftarrow\lambda_{i}I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{i}\leftarrow\lambda_{i}I</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg5.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg5.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
              
              
            Compute <math id="alg5.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as in (<a href="#S3.E17" title="In Proposition 1. ‣ 3.1 SDP-based Lipschitz Estimates with General Slope Bounds and Arbitrary Input-Output Indices ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ ECLipsE-Gen-Local: Efficient Compositional Local Lipschitz Estimates for Deep Neural Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>) using <math id="alg5.l13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Lambda}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Lambda}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg5.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg5.l14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">14:</span></span>
              
              
            Compute <math id="alg5.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}\leftarrow 1\big/\sigma_{\max}\!\left(W_{i+1}\,(M_{i})^{-1}\,W_{i+1}^{T}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}\leftarrow 1\big/\sigma_{\max}\!\left(W_{i+1}\,(M_{i})^{-1}\,W_{i+1}^{T}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg5.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg5.l15.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">15:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg5.l15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg5.l15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Lambda_{i},c_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Lambda_{i},c_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Experimental Details</h3>

<section id="A1.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">A.3.1 </span>Computational Resources</h4>

<div id="A1.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All algorithms except LipDiff are implemented on a Windows laptop with a 12-core CPU and 16 GB of RAM. LipDiff is accelerated using a compute node equipped with a single NVIDIA A100 GPU (80 GB onboard memory) and 512 GB of system RAM.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">A.3.2 </span>Randomly Generated Neural Networks</h4>

<div id="A1.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the experiments in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Scalability, Efficiency, and Tightness on Randomly Generated Networks ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ ECLipsE-Gen-Local: Efficient Compositional Local Lipschitz Estimates for Deep Neural Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, network weights are generated such that the <math id="A1.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm of each layer weight lies in <math id="A1.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0.8,\,2.5]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0.8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 2.5</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0.8,\,2.5]</annotation></semantics></math>. This is accomplished by first sampling a target value uniformly from <math id="A1.SS3.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0.8,\,2.5]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0.8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 2.5</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0.8,\,2.5]</annotation></semantics></math> for each layer, and then normalizing the randomly generated weight matrix. Similarly, for the networks in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Tightness of Local Estimates: Achieving Provable Upper Bounds at autodiff Level ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ ECLipsE-Gen-Local: Efficient Compositional Local Lipschitz Estimates for Deep Neural Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, the <math id="A1.SS3.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm of each layer weight is constrained to <math id="A1.SS3.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[2,\,2.5]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 2.5</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[2,\,2.5]</annotation></semantics></math>.
When applying Algorithm EClipsE-Gen-Local-Acc, we set <math id="A1.SS3.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{i}=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{i}=100</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E31" title="In 3.4.2 Numerical Instability in Degenerate Slope Bounds ‣ 3.4 Acceleration and Stability Safeguards ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ ECLipsE-Gen-Local: Efficient Compositional Local Lipschitz Estimates for Deep Neural Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">31</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">A.3.3 </span>MNIST Training and Robustness Evaluation</h4>

<div id="A1.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate adversarial robustness on the MNIST dataset, which consists of <math id="A1.SS3.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="28\times 28" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>28</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>28</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">28\times 28</annotation></semantics></math> grayscale images of handwritten digits from 0 to 9. Each image is vectorized into a 784-dimensional input, and the networks output a 10-dimensional vector corresponding to the ten digit classes. All feedforward networks used in this experiment therefore have input size <math id="A1.SS3.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="784" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>784</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">784</annotation></semantics></math> and output size <math id="A1.SS3.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>. The models are trained with Adam (learning rate <math id="A1.SS3.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>, weight decay <math id="A1.SS3.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>) for up to 50 epochs with early stopping at 98% test accuracy. The baseline uses cross-entropy loss, while the Jacobian regularized model adds a Frobenius norm penalty estimated with one Hutchinson probe (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hoffman et al. (2019)</a></cite>) and penalizing weight <math id="A1.SS3.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Adversarial robustness is measured using projected gradient descent (PGD) attacks. Given an image <math id="A1.SS3.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> with label <math id="A1.SS3.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, we optimize the cross-entropy loss with respect to a perturbation <math id="A1.SS3.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> subject to the <math id="A1.SS3.SSS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> constraint <math id="A1.SS3.SSS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\delta\|_{2}\leq\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\delta\|_{2}\leq\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. Starting from a small randomized initialization, we perform 40 steps of gradient ascent with normalized gradients and step size <math id="A1.SS3.SSS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\varepsilon/10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\varepsilon/10</annotation></semantics></math>. After each step, the perturbed input <math id="A1.SS3.SSS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x+\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x+\delta</annotation></semantics></math> is projected back onto the <math id="A1.SS3.SSS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> ball of radius <math id="A1.SS3.SSS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and clipped to the valid pixel range <math id="A1.SS3.SSS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]^{784}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>784</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]^{784}</annotation></semantics></math>. We sweep <math id="A1.SS3.SSS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\in\{1/2,1/4,1/8,1/16,1/32,1/64,1/128,1/256\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\in\{1/2,1/4,1/8,1/16,1/32,1/64,1/128,1/256\}</annotation></semantics></math> and report the <em id="A1.SS3.SSS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">failure rate</em>, defined as the fraction of test examples for which the classifier prediction changes under attack.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">A.3.4 </span>Complete Experimental Results</h4>

<div id="A1.SS3.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.SSS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 1 of Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Scalability, Efficiency, and Tightness on Randomly Generated Networks ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ ECLipsE-Gen-Local: Efficient Compositional Local Lipschitz Estimates for Deep Neural Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>:</span> The Lipschitz constant estimates and computation times for the randomly generated neural networks with the number of layers chosen from <math id="A1.SS3.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{5,10,15,20,25\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{5,10,15,20,25\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and number of neurons chosen from <math id="A1.SS3.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{10,20,40,60\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>60</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{10,20,40,60\}</annotation></semantics></math> (small neural networks), are provided below.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span id="A1.SS3.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{longtblr}</span>
